// The guard's shared test vectors: hooks/guard.ts (tests/guard.test.ts)
// and its Go port (internal/guard/guard_test.go, TestVectors) both
// pass every case: what the call breaks (rule, null for none) and the
// summary journaled; and the sentence each rule gives each role. The
// tools (each one's kind and input fields, as a manifest's [guard.tools]
// gives them and GET /v1/rules serves them) go with every rule set:
// Claude's and Codex's, which their manifests must match
// (internal/agent TestGuardToolVectors), and a lowercase vocabulary like
// pi's and opencode's. A case the guard asks about rather than refuses
// (a PowerShell command it can't read) has rule "unknown" and "ask": true.
// The thread-mods and mods-only sets carry rules a mod adds (mods design
// §7, Tighten only): the core's and the mod's, and the mod's alone.
// The yolo-thread set is a yolo thread's guard: the floor only, with
// tm's settings and the project folder no tool writes (noWrite); the
// floor-coordinator set the coordinator's, whose project folder it
// writes but for tm's parts of it; thread-escapes asks about a call that
// asks to leave the sandbox (askEscapes), rule "escape"; thread-ask-first
// asks about a command of an excluded command that asks first (askFirst),
// rule "excluded", and thread-ask-first-only has nothing else; thread-own
// and thread-own-off carry excluded commands of the user's own Claude
// settings (unsandboxed): a call one takes and tm's (tmExcluded) don't is
// an escape, asked, or with escapes off (noEscapes) refused.
// The thread-allow, thread-learn and floor-only sets carry the human's
// allowances (a refusal under an allowance's rule of the command line or
// file it matches is lifted; an "exact" one's * matches only a *), tm's
// own files and the secret variables:
// a case with "floor" is a refusal no allowance lifts; the rules-off
// sets have the force-push and merge rules off (guard_off) and their
// floor still refusing; "subject" (the
// command line or file an allowance names) and "message", when a case
// gives them, are the refusal's.
// A case with links judges file writes where they land: each link (an
// absolute path) leads to its target, and a write under a link to the
// same place under the target (the server's POST /v1/resolve, faked).
// A case with files has those paths (absolute, clean, where they land)
// regular files, the rest not: a shell given input runs a script of the
// worktree only where one is (POST /v1/resolve's files, faked).
// Everything after "export default " is JSON, for the Go test: keep it
// so (double quotes, no trailing commas, no comments). One case a line.
